ackground is microwave radiation that fills all space in the observable universe."}, {"id": "B", "text": "The CMB is the key experimental evidence of the Big Bang theory for the origin of the universe."}, {"id": "C", "text": "The accidental discovery of the CMB in 1964 was the culmination of work initiated in the 1940s."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Its energy density exceeds that of all the photons emitted by all the stars in the history of the universe."}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'wikipedia-cc-by-sa-4', 'Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0)', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Wikipedia, "Cosmic microwave background", CC BY-SA 4.0, retrieved 2026-11-02. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cosmic_microwave_background', FALSE, 'active') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET external_id = EXCLUDED.external_id, assessments = EXCLUDED.assessments, section = EXCLUDED.section, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, skill = EXCLUDED.skill, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty, format = EXCLUDED.format, stimulus = EXCLUDED.stimulus, prompt = EXCLUDED.prompt, choices = EXCLUDED.choices, figure_metadata = EXCLUDED.figure_metadata, source_id = EXCLUDED.source_id, source_name = EXCLUDED.source_name, license = EXCLUDED.license, attribution = EXCLUDED.attribution, is_released_practice_set = EXCLUDED.is_released_practice_set, status = 'active';
INSERT INTO public.question_keys (question_id, correct_answer, acceptable_answers, explanation, distractor_rationales) VALUES ('RW-M30-CE009', 'B', ARRAY['B']::TEXT[], 'The correct quotation explicitly calls the CMB key evidence for the Big Bang.', '{"A": "This sentence defines the CMB.", "C": "This sentence describes its discovery.", "D": "This sentence compares its energy density."}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT (question_id) DO UPDATE SET correct_answer = EXCLUDED.correct_answer, acceptable_answers = EXCLUDED.acceptable_answers, explanation = EXCLUDED.explanation, distractor_rationales = EXCLUDED.distractor_rationales;
INSERT INTO public.questions (id, external_id, assessments, section, domain, skill, difficulty, format, stimulus, prompt, choices, figure_metadata, source_id, source_name, license, attribution, is_released_practice_set, status) VALUES ('RW-M30-CI010', 'HDSAT-RW-M30-CI010', ARRAY['SAT', 'PSAT/NMSQT', 'PSAT 10', 'PSAT 8/9']::TEXT[], 'Reading and Writing', 'Information and Ideas', 'Central Ideas and Details', 'Medium', 'multiple_choice', 'An asteroid is a minor planet that orbits within the inner Solar System or is co-orbital with Jupiter (Trojan asteroids). These objects are larger than a meteoroid (thus 1 meter or larger) and are neither a planet nor an identified comet. Asteroids are rocky, metallic, or icy bodies with no atmosphere, and are broadly classified into C-type (carbonaceous), M-type (metallic), or S-type (silicaceous). The size and shape of asteroids vary significantly, ranging from small rubble piles under a kilometer across to Ceres, a dwarf planet almost 1000 km in diameter. A body is classified as a comet, not an asteroid, if it shows a coma (tail) when warmed by solar radiation, although recent observations suggest a continuum between these types of bodies. Of the roughly one million known asteroids, the greatest number are located between the orbits of Mars and Jupiter, approximately 2 to 4 astronomical units (AU) from the Sun, in a region known as the main asteroid belt. The total mass of all the asteroids combined is only 3% that of Earth''s Moon. The majority of main belt asteroids follow slightly elliptical, stable orbits, revolving in the same direction as the Earth and taking from three to six years to complete a full orbit around the Sun. Asteroids have been observed from Earth since their discovery in 1801. The first close-up observation of an asteroid was made by the Galileo spacecraft.', 'Which choice best states the main idea of the text?', '[{"id": "A", "text": "The asteroids'' total mass exceeds Earth''s."}, {"id": "B", "text": "Asteroids are large planets with atmospheres."}, {"id": "C", "text": "Most asteroids orbit beyond Neptune."}, {"id": "D", "text": "Asteroids are rocky, metallic, or icy minor planets, most orbiting between Mars and Jupiter, with total mass far below the Moon''s."}]'::jsonb, NULL, 'wikipedia-cc-by-sa-4', 'Wikipedia (CC BY-SA 4.0)', 'CC BY-SA 4.0', 'Wikipedia, "Asteroid", CC BY-SA 4.0, retrieved 2026-11-02. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Asteroid', FALSE, 'active') ON CONFLICT (id) DO UPDATE SET external_id = EXCLUDED.external_id, assessments = EXCLUDED.assessments, section = EXCLUDED.section, domain = EXCLUDED.domain, skill = EXCLUDED.skill, difficulty = EXCLUDED.difficulty, format = EXCLUDED.format, stimulus = EXCLUDED.stimulus, prompt = EXCLUDED.prompt, choices = EXCLUDED.choices, figure_metadata = EXCLUDED.figure_metadata, source_id = EXCLUDED.source_id, source_name = EXCLUDED.source_name, license = EXCLUDED.license, attribution = EXCLUDED.attribution, is_released_practice_set = EXCLUDED.is_released_practice_set, status = 'active';
INSERT INTO public.question_keys (question_id, correct_answer, acceptable_answers, explanation, distractor_rationales) VALUES ('RW-M30-CI010', 'D', ARRAY['D']::TEXT[], 'The passage defines asteroids, classifies them, and describes their location, mass, and orbits.', '{"B": "The text says asteroids have no atmosphere and are rocky, metallic, or icy.", "C": "The text says the greatest number are in the main belt between Mars and Jupiter.", "A": "The text says their combined mass is 3% of the Moon''s."}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT (question_id) DO UPDATE SET correct_answer = EXCLUDED.correct_answer, acceptable_answers = EXCLUDED.acceptable_answers, explanation = EXCLUDED.explanation, distractor_rationales = EXCLUDED.distractor_rationales;
INSERT INTO public.questions (id, external_id, assessments, section, domain, skill, difficulty, format, stimulus, prompt, choices, figure_metadata, source_id, source_name, license, attribution, is_released_practice_set, status) VALUES ('RW-M30-INF010', 'HDSAT-RW-M30-INF010', ARRAY['SAT', 'PSAT/NMSQT', 'PSAT 10', 'PSAT 8/9']::TEXT[], 'Reading and Writing', 'Information and Ideas', 'Inferences', 'Medium', 'multiple_choice', 'An asteroid is a minor planet that orbits within the inner Solar System or is co-orbital with Jupiter (Trojan asteroids). These objects are larger than a meteoroid (thus 1 meter or larger) and are neither a planet nor an identified comet. Asteroids are rocky, metallic, or icy bodies with no atmosphere, and are broadly classified into C-type (carbonaceous), M-type (metallic), or S-type (silicaceous). The size and shape of asteroids vary significantly, ranging from small rubble piles under a kilometer across to Ceres, a dwarf planet almost 1000 km in diameter. A body is classified as a comet, not an asteroid, if it shows a coma (tail) when warmed by solar radiation, although recent observations suggest a continuum between these types of bodies. Of the roughly one million known asteroids, the greatest number are located between the orbits of Mars and Jupiter, approximately 2 to 4 astronomical units (AU) from the Sun, in a region known as the main asteroid belt. The total mass of all the asteroids combined is only 3% that of Earth''s Moon. The majority of main belt asteroids follow slightly elliptical, stable orbits, revolving in the same direction as the Earth and taking from three to six years to complete a full orbit around the Sun. Asteroids have been observed from Earth since their discovery in 1801. The first close-up observation of an asteroid was made by the Galileo spacecraft.
INSERT INTO public.question_keys (question_id, correct_answer, acceptable_answers, explanation, distractor_rationales) VALUES ('RW-M30-INF010', 'B', ARRAY['B']::TEXT[], 'The passage explicitly uses a coma as the distinction between comet and asteroid.', '{"D": "The text states that a body showing a coma when warmed is classified as a comet.", "A": "Planets are defined separately from minor bodies.", "C": "Visibility is not the criterion described."}'::jsonb) ON CONFLICT (question_id) DO UPDATE SET correct_answer = EXCLUDED.correct_answer, acceptable_answers = EXCLUDED.acceptable_answers, explanation = EXCLUDED.explanation, distractor_rationales = EXCLUDED.distractor_rationales;
